00:14:25,840 --> 00:14:26,960
So that's where that came in.

153
00:14:27,280 --> 00:14:28,400
What is washi tape?

154
00:14:28,640 --> 00:14:32,240
Well, washi tape is like Japanese paper tape.

155
00:14:32,560 --> 00:14:39,520
So it comes in a whole lot of different patterns and yeah, washi is the type of paper.

156
00:14:40,080 --> 00:14:42,960
Basically there's a heaps of different patterns and colors.

157
00:14:43,520 --> 00:14:46,480
It's like a crafting yeah, it's a crafting tool.

158
00:14:46,880 --> 00:14:55,520
Okay, so this is like this is what those kind of banners you're using to like separate the different, I guess, modules or capsicum components.

159
00:14:55,920 --> 00:14:56,640
Yeah, that's right.

160
00:14:56,880 --> 00:15:01,360
We're using it both for decoration and to divide up, to divide up content.

161
00:15:01,840 --> 00:15:02,400
That's really cool.

162
00:15:03,040 --> 00:15:04,640
So you said this took like three years.

163
00:15:04,960 --> 00:15:05,680
So yeah.

164
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:08,560
You've gone through like a bunch of different design iterations then?

165
00:15:08,880 --> 00:15:09,440
Yes, we have.

166
00:15:11,280 --> 00:15:31,840
For like the first year, it was basically me and Ish testing it on our own devices, like we were we were using it for ourselves, so like adding our own to-dos and notes and we have loose-leaf pages where you can add notes and to-dos as well that aren't attached to a particular date or time.

167
00:15:32,720 --> 00:15:36,160
So we were just using it and testing it out how we wanted to use it.

168
00:15:36,720 --> 00:15:38,000
And it evolved over time.

169
00:15:38,640 --> 00:15:46,080
Later on, we added habit tracking to it as well because we're like, that's a big part of what people do in their paper planers.

170
00:15:47,280 --> 00:15:56,320
They actually track things like whether they drank water or what movies they watched or other things they want to achieve, like cooking dinner every night.

171
00:15:56,720 --> 00:16:00,640
So we decided to add a habit tracking component into it.

172
00:16:01,200 --> 00:16:03,920
So is that, you said other people are doing that?

173
00:16:04,080 --> 00:16:13,680
Is, are you like talking to other people and just trying to like ask around like how do you use your planner or did you guys start like a beta testing period around that time?

174
00:16:14,560 --> 00:16:16,080
We did start beta testing.

175
00:16:17,040 --> 00:16:29,920
My research mostly involved like looking at Pinterest and Instagram posts of people's like bullet journals and notebooks, how they like to decorate them and the kind of things they put in there.

176
00:16:30,080 --> 00:16:32,000
I watched a few YouTube videos as well.

177
00:16:33,680 --> 00:16:48,400
I have not been someone who's done bullet journaling myself and bullet journaling is it's a simple method of planning that can become more complex and decorative if an individual wants to.

178
00:16:49,120 --> 00:17:02,400
But at its premise, it's like simple, like different bullets represent different things, like a circle with a line around it that's not colored in represents a task that has to be done.

179
00:17:03,120 --> 00:17:06,800
If it's colored in, so it's a solid bullet, then it's done.

180
00:17:07,920 --> 00:17:12,960
If there's an arrow through the bullet, that means you're moving it to another day to do it.

181
00:17:13,200 --> 00:17:17,360
And like people can kind of customize those bullets a bit for how they want to do it.

182
00:17:17,680 --> 00:17:19,680
Okay, so that's why it's called bullet journalism.

183
00:17:19,840 --> 00:17:19,920
Okay.

184
00:17:20,160 --> 00:17:22,880
I never actually put that together, but that seems obvious now that you say that.

185
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:26,400
I hope I described bullet journaling okay there.

186
00:17:27,920 --> 00:17:37,600
As I'm not a big bullet journaler myself, I'm not down with all the details, but it was a bit of an inspiration in building Capsicum.

187
00:17:38,160 --> 00:17:49,360
Yeah, that's interesting, because that's one of those things that's always weird about software development in general is like often, especially with Indie apps, you're kind of building something for yourself.

188
00:17:49,760 --> 00:17:49,840
Yeah.

189
00:17:50,240 --> 00:18:00,640
But then when you start opening it up to the world, you realize everyone else is using it differently than you, and you kind of have to learn about a bunch of things that you personally don't care about yourself.

190
00:18:01,280 --> 00:18:13,600
And it sounds like with this because it was sort of a co creation thing, almost from the get-go, you were having to do that sort of leg work to learn about a world that you weren't necessarily personally familiar with.

191
00:18:14,160 --> 00:18:14,560
That's right.

192
00:18:15,200 --> 00:18:24,400
However, I am always like looking for ways to be more productive because there's so much stuff in my head that I have to get out.

193
00:18:24,720 --> 00:18:43,120
And I've tried so many different systems like Trello and JIRA and Todoist and a whole like I've tried a whole bunch of different apps to try and get out what's in my head and to plan what I have to do.

194
00:18:43,520 --> 00:18:55,360
So that's where Capsicon also came in creating a system that would work for me and for other people who are similarly interested in this kind of planning.

195
00:18:55,760 --> 00:19:05,840
Yeah, that that's one question I had was with working with another person, but also working with another person on like opposite ends of the time zone.

196
00:19:06,480 --> 00:19:09,520
How did you guys like manage tasks?

197
00:19:10,160 --> 00:19:10,800
That's a good question.

198
00:19:12,160 --> 00:19:17,680
Well, firstly, I'm very flexible with my timetable.

